sip: a TLS next hop given by IP address may prove the dialog's host name

A request inside a dialog follows the dialog's route, and servers
commonly name themselves there by a bare IP address (a Record-Route
with an advertised address). When no connection to that address is
open, a new TLS connection is verified against the IP address, which
certificates rarely carry, so the request (a BYE, a re-INVITE, a REFER)
is never sent.

A dialog now keeps the host name this side configured for it:

- a dialog this side creates takes the host of its initial route (the
  outbound proxy or the target; its maddr when present), unless that is
  an IP address;
- a dialog created from a received request takes the name of the
  dialog this side opened the request's TLS connection for.

A connection carries a name only when it was opened for a dialog's own
host or for an IP-address hop of that dialog; one opened to a host a
peer named carries none. So the name is never taken from a peer's
message, and it never changes.

When a request of the dialog goes over TLS to a next hop given by IP
address, a new connection accepts a certificate valid for the address,
as before, or for that name, and sends the name as SNI, which no
connection to an IP address did before (tls_set_verify_server_alt).
Chain, trust and validity checks are unchanged. A next hop given by
host name is verified against that name as before.

X509_check_host() is deprecated from OpenSSL 4.1; the call in
tls_verify_handler_alt() needs its successor when the build moves there.

Origin: written for baresip-python; not yet offered upstream.
libre is BSD-3-Clause; this patch is offered under the same terms.

